  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: First the heat, then the storms. Temperatures \nacross the CWA have risen in the mid to upper 90s resulting in heat \nindices greater than 105F across the west and southern portions of \nthe CWA and near 105F elsewhere. These oppressive conditions will \ncontinue until this evening unless being knocked down by rain cooled \nair. Local radars were not showing much activity in our CWA at early \nafternoon but the remnants of a cluster of storms shifting east \nacross northeast Mississippi will drop an outflow boundary to the \nsouth over our eastern zones. In addition, there was a disturbance \ngenerating convection across northern Arkansas that was dropping \nsouth. The development in our eastern zones may develop sooner than \nthe convection to our northwest but considering the thermal profiles \nand anticipated shear, and storm that develops will have the \npotential for damaging wind gusts and locally heavy rainfall. The \nrain will be beneficial and there may be minor runoff issues in the \nurban and poor drainage areas but flash flooding is not a concern. \nThe greatest coverage of severe storms tonight is expected to occur \nacross our northern and eastern zones. Provided the areas of storms \ncome together as forecast, rain will be ongoing across our southern \nzones Thursday morning including cloud debris over most of our CWA. \nThis will make it difficult to anticipate the extent of heat stress \nThursday afternoon. The hottest temperatures and humidity will \nlikely occur over our delta region where peak heat index values may \nreach or exceed 105F again. /22/ \n\nThursday Night through next Tuesday...The ongoing northwest flow \npattern across the region will persist through the end of the work \nweek. This will continue to provide decent rain chances across \nportions of the forecast area, mainly southeast of the Natchez Trace \nParkway, as a boundary combines with heating in this moist and \nunstable airmass on Friday. Although storms are currently expected \nto remain below severe limits, the most intense storms will be \ncapable of producing gusty winds. Frequent lightning and heavy \ndownpours are possible with any convection that develops Friday \nafternoon into early Friday evening.\n\nOver the weekend and into early next week, high pressure aloft will \nbegin to re-assert itself across the Lower Mississippi River Valley. \nThis will result in lessening rain chances across the forecast area. \nUnfortunately, the trade-off will again be a gradual increase in \nheat, but some isolated convection will be possible each afternoon. \nBy next Tuesday, some need for at least a \"Limited\" threat of \nincreased heat stress may exist across portions of the forecast \narea. /19/",
